But for us, we really focused
on understanding not just

00:11:38.960 --> 00:11:40.460
how the product
worked but also what

00:11:40.460 --> 00:11:41.627
didn't work for the product.

00:11:41.627 --> 00:11:44.300
Because it wasn't
important for us to pick it

00:11:44.300 --> 00:11:47.150
if it worked perfectly but
rather, just like any business

00:11:47.150 --> 00:11:49.160
today, what's down
the roadmap, what

00:11:49.160 --> 00:11:50.810
can we use today,
how do we set it

00:11:50.810 --> 00:11:53.570
up so that we can work
around any potential issues.

00:11:53.570 --> 00:11:55.370
And that doesn't
happen in a silo.

00:11:55.370 --> 00:11:58.370
So any partnership
for any migration

00:11:58.370 --> 00:12:01.100
requires that you
actually invest the time,

00:12:01.100 --> 00:12:03.710
as part of your
pre-migration strategy,

00:12:03.710 --> 00:12:07.550
to really learn with the
team about what they have

00:12:07.550 --> 00:12:08.780
and how it works.

00:12:08.780 --> 00:12:13.760
SOM ROY: So if you look across
the different Unity business

00:12:13.760 --> 00:12:16.880
units across the stack,
they're using a lot of products

00:12:16.880 --> 00:12:18.020
right now.

00:12:18.020 --> 00:12:20.120
Even after the
migration was complete,

00:12:20.120 --> 00:12:22.550
newer work teams are
getting kicked in.

00:12:22.550 --> 00:12:24.800
I think 2018-- correct
me, Venk, if I'm

00:12:24.800 --> 00:12:28.100
wrong-- was a focus was really,
really on lift and shift,

00:12:28.100 --> 00:12:30.410
move the workloads over to GCP.

00:12:30.410 --> 00:12:33.800
2019 is more around
enhancing the data,

00:12:33.800 --> 00:12:35.760
looking at email use cases.

00:12:35.760 --> 00:12:38.750
So I would say last year
was very, very focused

00:12:38.750 --> 00:12:41.760
when we did the migration
around compute, storage,

00:12:41.760 --> 00:12:44.810
networking, all of the core
foundations of the stack.

00:12:44.810 --> 00:12:47.870
And 2019, again, as I
said, the story only

00:12:47.870 --> 00:12:50.390
starts after the migration
when the workloads actually

00:12:50.390 --> 00:12:51.230
come over.

00:12:51.230 --> 00:12:53.600
So this year, we are
actually working with Unity

00:12:53.600 --> 00:12:57.630
to enhance that and spend our
time in the data and email

00:12:57.630 --> 00:12:58.130
space.

00:12:58.130 --> 00:12:59.713
VENK SUBRAMANIAN:
And once again, this

00:12:59.713 --> 00:13:01.850
was not a generic
decision that we took

00:13:01.850 --> 00:13:03.530
by looking at the technologies.

00:13:03.530 --> 00:13:05.510
We look deeply within
our architecture

00:13:05.510 --> 00:13:09.530
and evaluated per situation
what makes sense to take over.

00:13:09.530 --> 00:13:11.120
It became very
quickly clear to us

00:13:11.120 --> 00:13:14.300
that compute was a central
piece of our technology.

00:13:14.300 --> 00:13:16.580
So it wasn't enough that
we just lift and shift it.

00:13:16.580 --> 00:13:19.370
We had to look at
Google-managed technologies

00:13:19.370 --> 00:13:22.790
and figure out what we could use
there in order for us to scale.

00:13:22.790 --> 00:13:25.430
On the other hand,
Unity has always

00:13:25.430 --> 00:13:28.190
been very, very
passionate about data

00:13:28.190 --> 00:13:29.700
and about machine learning.

00:13:29.700 --> 00:13:33.470
But those were things that were
very customized within Unity

00:13:33.470 --> 00:13:33.992
to date.

00:13:33.992 --> 00:13:36.200
So in that case, it didn't
actually make sense for us

00:13:36.200 --> 00:13:39.770
to try to bring it over
within the Google setup

00:13:39.770 --> 00:13:41.630
because a
re-architecture would be

00:13:41.630 --> 00:13:44.400
too much for us to handle at
the same time as a migration.

00:13:44.400 --> 00:13:46.250
So we looked at each
piece individually

00:13:46.250 --> 00:13:48.700
to make a decision about
how we had to move it over.

00:13:48.700 --> 00:13:50.983
SOM ROY: So why was
this important, Venk?

00:13:50.983 --> 00:13:52.150
Can you give you an example?

00:13:52.150 --> 00:13:54.108
VENK SUBRAMANIAN: So I
don't know if any of you

00:13:54.108 --> 00:13:58.550
know this game "Apex
Legends," but once we finish

00:13:58.550 --> 00:14:03.410
the migration over, we had
our multiplayer platform now

00:14:03.410 --> 00:14:05.960
able to support
workloads through GCP.

00:14:05.960 --> 00:14:09.140
And when "Apex" launched,
it was a huge launch.

00:14:09.140 --> 00:14:11.060
For those of you
that remember it,

00:14:11.060 --> 00:14:13.160
they had over one
million players

00:14:13.160 --> 00:14:15.140
within the first couple of days.

00:14:15.140 --> 00:14:17.510
Today, they have over
50 million players

00:14:17.510 --> 00:14:21.320
in and out of the game,
2.3 million simultaneously.

00:14:21.320 --> 00:14:22.890
And this is around the world.

00:14:22.890 --> 00:14:27.470
So at peak times, we're actually
using 230,000 compute cores.

00:14:27.470 --> 00:14:29.860
I mean, pretty sure we've
maxed out the Google quota

00:14:29.860 --> 00:14:31.960
multiple times
through this process.

00:14:31.960 --> 00:14:35.950
But it was possible to really
quickly launch this and scale

00:14:35.950 --> 00:14:39.700
it because the backing and the
foundation of GCP and the way

00:14:39.700 --> 00:14:41.757
that we architected
it was solid.

00:14:41.757 --> 00:14:42.340
SOM ROY: Yeah.

00:14:42.340 --> 00:14:43.570
This is really important.

00:14:43.570 --> 00:14:46.420
Like Google Cloud with scale
up, and it will scale up

00:14:46.420 --> 00:14:48.910
to handle such launches.

00:14:48.910 --> 00:14:50.920
But again, because
we kind of set up

00:14:50.920 --> 00:14:54.550
the networking and
IM, and the security,

00:14:54.550 --> 00:14:57.940
collaboration with the Unity
team, that just adding cores

00:14:57.940 --> 00:15:00.550
was relatively the easier
part of the problem

00:15:00.550 --> 00:15:01.960
once we set up the foundation.

00:15:01.960 --> 00:15:03.830
Then again, we keep
harping on that point

00:15:03.830 --> 00:15:05.505
because, I think
both for me and Venk,

00:15:05.505 --> 00:15:06.880
that is really
close to our heart

00:15:06.880 --> 00:15:09.630
that we spent that time
in the first quarter.

00:15:09.630 --> 00:15:11.230
VENK SUBRAMANIAN:
So let's dig really

00:15:11.230 --> 00:15:15.220
deep into how we actually
planned this migration.

00:15:15.220 --> 00:15:17.080
So here's the background.

00:15:17.080 --> 00:15:19.120
Now, we talked about
the partnership.

00:15:19.120 --> 00:15:20.775
The partnership was
not just a PR spiel

00:15:20.775 --> 00:15:23.150
that Som and I came up with
because our companies told us

00:15:23.150 --> 00:15:23.660
to.

00:15:23.660 --> 00:15:25.240
The partnership
is important here

00:15:25.240 --> 00:15:27.670
to mention because
we actually had

00:15:27.670 --> 00:15:30.370
to take this vision not
just to the executives

00:15:30.370 --> 00:15:32.350
about the importance
of this partnership,

00:15:32.350 --> 00:15:35.260
but you actually have to do
that for each one of your teams.

00:15:35.260 --> 00:15:40.180
You cannot expect your teams to
believe and move independently

00:15:40.180 --> 00:15:44.320
on something as key as a
migration unless you explain

00:15:44.320 --> 00:15:47.930
to them the vision, the
goals, and the business value.

00:15:47.930 --> 00:15:50.050
And if you're not doing
that with your teams,

00:15:50.050 --> 00:15:53.180
then you're not empowering them
to be part of the business.

00:15:53.180 --> 00:15:55.120
So when we set up
this partnership.

00:15:55.120 --> 00:15:56.680
We took that to the teams.

00:15:56.680 --> 00:15:59.630
And we empowered them by
explaining the problem.

00:15:59.630 --> 00:16:01.420
And after that, we
got out of the way.

00:16:01.420 --> 00:16:03.190
We empowered them
with the problem

00:16:03.190 --> 00:16:05.350
and asked them to come up
with the right solution.

00:16:05.350 --> 00:16:07.870
We told them all of
the business needs,

00:16:07.870 --> 00:16:11.230
the deadlines, what happens
if we go over x versus y,

00:16:11.230 --> 00:16:13.330
how is spend affected
through this year.

00:16:13.330 --> 00:16:14.830
We laid it out for the teams.

00:16:14.830 --> 00:16:17.330
And when I say teams, I don't
just mean the directors.

00:16:17.330 --> 00:16:19.660
I mean every
engineer, every lead,

00:16:19.660 --> 00:16:21.730
had access to this
information and was

00:16:21.730 --> 00:16:23.890
encouraged to know about it.

00:16:23.890 --> 00:16:25.780
When you do that,
now you have teams

00:16:25.780 --> 00:16:27.610
that are truly bought
in because they're

00:16:27.610 --> 00:16:29.170
aligned with that vision.

00:16:29.170 --> 00:16:32.800
And then it becomes a
self-fulfilling prophecy.

00:16:32.800 --> 00:16:35.470
The second thing we did was
we used this as an opportunity

00:16:35.470 --> 00:16:37.042
for evaluation and refresh.

00:16:37.042 --> 00:16:39.250
One of the things that you
hear a lot in the industry

00:16:39.250 --> 00:16:42.250
is lift and shift or
transformation, right?

00:16:42.250 --> 00:16:44.680
And it becomes this binary
thing that you're solving for.

00:16:44.680 --> 00:16:47.012
You're either taking
all of your crap

00:16:47.012 --> 00:16:48.970
that you have in your
house and moving it over,

00:16:48.970 --> 00:16:51.460
or you're just scrapping
it entirely and just buying

00:16:51.460 --> 00:16:52.340
everything new.

00:16:52.340 --> 00:16:54.160
But that's not
really true, is it?

00:16:54.160 --> 00:16:56.240
Every situation is unique.

00:16:56.240 --> 00:16:58.180
You have to look
at every scenario

00:16:58.180 --> 00:17:02.320
and evaluate using the
guidelines and the goals

00:17:02.320 --> 00:17:05.200
that you've outlined as
part of the empowerment

00:17:05.200 --> 00:17:07.040
to understand what makes sense.

00:17:07.040 --> 00:17:09.460
So for example, in
our case, we had been

00:17:09.460 --> 00:17:11.680
using our own Kubernetes stack.

00:17:11.680 --> 00:17:13.780
But we took the time
to evaluate GKE,

00:17:13.780 --> 00:17:15.460
and we realized
the value that it

00:17:15.460 --> 00:17:19.250
was going to bring because what
we had was essentially many,

00:17:19.250 --> 00:17:21.579
many sets of isolated
Kubernetes clusters.

00:17:21.579 --> 00:17:23.890
But what GKE allowed
us to do was really

00:17:23.890 --> 00:17:26.109
centralize that
system, and it actually

00:17:26.109 --> 00:17:29.050
fundamentally changed how we
operate as an organization.

00:17:29.050 --> 00:17:33.310
Today, instead of having every
team figure out their own GKE,

00:17:33.310 --> 00:17:35.170
we've centralized
these things back

00:17:35.170 --> 00:17:37.840
to teams that are
dedicated to it.

00:17:37.840 --> 00:17:40.870
As in, they are experts in GKE.

00:17:40.870 --> 00:17:42.460
They centralized the modules.

00:17:42.460 --> 00:17:43.870
They roll out the clusters.

00:17:43.870 --> 00:17:46.750
We use different models
to help other teams scale.

00:17:46.750 --> 00:17:49.270
But when we made that
decision, it actually

00:17:49.270 --> 00:17:50.675
helped us move faster.

00:17:50.675 --> 00:17:52.300
We did the same thing
with logging too.

00:17:52.300 --> 00:17:54.820
We had an in-house
stack that we had always

00:17:54.820 --> 00:17:56.560
been a little
troubled by because it

00:17:56.560 --> 00:17:58.080
wasn't super effective.

00:17:58.080 --> 00:18:01.930
When we looked at Stackdriver,
we evaluated the advantages

00:18:01.930 --> 00:18:05.942
we get in terms of gained
people back into the workforce

00:18:05.942 --> 00:18:07.900
because they don't have
to worry about managing

00:18:07.900 --> 00:18:10.810
an in-house stack versus what
it would cost us to run it.

00:18:10.810 --> 00:18:12.310
These are real things.

00:18:12.310 --> 00:18:14.860
These are practical things
that each team should not

00:18:14.860 --> 00:18:15.640
be looking at.

00:18:15.640 --> 00:18:18.070
It shouldn't just be happening
at the executive level.

00:18:18.070 --> 00:18:20.500
On the flip side, we have
a highly customized data

00:18:20.500 --> 00:18:21.400
pipeline.

00:18:21.400 --> 00:18:23.950
And that was not something that
we wanted to take to chance.

00:18:23.950 --> 00:18:27.130
We evaluated Pub/Sub
and our evaluation

00:18:27.130 --> 00:18:29.085
was that we're going
to move it over,

00:18:29.085 --> 00:18:31.460
or we're going to evaluate
how to move it over next year.

00:18:31.460 --> 00:18:33.790
So that one, we actually
knew because we'd

00:18:33.790 --> 00:18:36.580
done the math behind it that
it made more sense for us

00:18:36.580 --> 00:18:38.510
to take as is.

00:18:38.510 --> 00:18:40.510
Now, one of the things
that was to our advantage

00:18:40.510 --> 00:18:42.980
was we were a
distributed architecture.

00:18:42.980 --> 00:18:45.280
We've kind of been
very microservice mesh

00:18:45.280 --> 00:18:46.630
from the beginning.

00:18:46.630 --> 00:18:49.210
Everything that we do is
API and message based.

00:18:49.210 --> 00:18:52.360
So essentially, we already had
a bunch of independent layers

00:18:52.360 --> 00:18:55.160
that we could figure out
how to migrate them over.

00:18:55.160 --> 00:18:58.190
Now, I call this out because
this worked to our advantage.

00:18:58.190 --> 00:19:01.180
But there are technologies that
you're working on that maybe

00:19:01.180 --> 00:19:03.460
a monolith, that maybe
multiple monoliths,

00:19:03.460 --> 00:19:06.940
or some version of a monolith
and a distributed architecture.

00:19:06.940 --> 00:19:08.440
These are important
because you have

00:19:08.440 --> 00:19:10.480
to do your own
independent evaluation.

00:19:10.480 --> 00:19:13.570
It's not just enough to look
outside and pick the standard

00:19:13.570 --> 00:19:16.740
that everybody else is using.

00:19:16.740 --> 00:19:20.410
And then, global teams, again,
we've talked about this before.

00:19:20.410 --> 00:19:23.290
This was a particularly
unique problem for us,

00:19:23.290 --> 00:19:27.010
which is we're trying to
do a migration in a very

00:19:27.010 --> 00:19:29.830
short timeline with teams
that are across the world

00:19:29.830 --> 00:19:31.730
and how do we coordinate that.

00:19:31.730 --> 00:19:35.320
So what we did, and I'm going
to cover this in the next slide

00:19:35.320 --> 00:19:37.240
in planning, what
we did essentially

00:19:37.240 --> 00:19:40.150
was really kind of
focus on the results

00:19:40.150 --> 00:19:41.500
rather than the process.

00:19:41.500 --> 00:19:43.510
We focused on
empowering these teams

00:19:43.510 --> 00:19:47.050
so that they could move on what
they needed to independently.

00:19:47.050 --> 00:19:50.300
But we had to come up with ways
to be able to track the system.

00:19:50.300 --> 00:19:52.720
And finally-- and this was
the toughest part-- business

00:19:52.720 --> 00:19:55.300
continuity during the migration.

00:19:55.300 --> 00:19:57.248
There was no stopping
for the business.

00:19:57.248 --> 00:19:59.290
We continued to work on
new products and features

00:19:59.290 --> 00:20:00.370
and rolled them out.

00:20:00.370 --> 00:20:04.540
We had no downtime while we
migrated a high-scale system.

00:20:04.540 --> 00:20:06.670
I mean, we have 2
to 3 billion users

00:20:06.670 --> 00:20:08.410
in and out of this
system every month.

00:20:08.410 --> 00:20:11.890
We processed 20,000,
30,000 events every second.

00:20:11.890 --> 00:20:14.110
And all of this
is at low latency.

00:20:14.110 --> 00:20:16.450
In some cases, the
latency requirements

00:20:16.450 --> 00:20:21.880
were so strong that we had to
pick data centers on both sides

00:20:21.880 --> 00:20:24.560
so that we could route
in under 10 milliseconds.

00:20:24.560 --> 00:20:27.760
And we did all of that without
experiencing any downtime.

00:20:27.760 --> 00:20:29.440
SOM ROY: And that,
I think, Venk,

00:20:29.440 --> 00:20:32.200
is another key point
that always comes up.

00:20:32.200 --> 00:20:36.040
Again, from the PSO side, we
always see the customer saying,

00:20:36.040 --> 00:20:38.500
or our engineer is going
to build the next product,

00:20:38.500 --> 00:20:40.720
or are they going to
focus on migration,

00:20:40.720 --> 00:20:42.970
because they are both
equally time taking.

00:20:42.970 --> 00:20:46.565
And while, on one side, you
have to make the business go

00:20:46.565 --> 00:20:48.190
forward, come up with
the new features,

00:20:48.190 --> 00:20:49.815
you also have to
finish this migration.

00:20:49.815 --> 00:20:52.900
So I think just the
balance that the Unity

00:20:52.900 --> 00:20:55.250
team, with the help
on the PSO side,

00:20:55.250 --> 00:20:58.120
we were able to create
was really, really a--

00:20:58.120 --> 00:21:00.430
that's one of the key things
why these things went well

00:21:00.430 --> 00:21:02.710
and we were able to complete
this within a short time frame.

00:21:02.710 --> 00:21:04.335
VENK SUBRAMANIAN:
And again, for us, it

00:21:04.335 --> 00:21:06.070
was a practical
decision because we

00:21:06.070 --> 00:21:07.930
looked at the logistics of it.

00:21:07.930 --> 00:21:09.580
If we're building a
feature, what value

00:21:09.580 --> 00:21:11.410
do we get with
differing sections

00:21:11.410 --> 00:21:12.910
of the migration
versus what do we

00:21:12.910 --> 00:21:15.400
get from speeding up the
migration in certain aspects?

00:21:15.400 --> 00:21:17.870
We always went
back to the basics.

00:21:17.870 --> 00:21:19.570
We always looked
at the goals when

00:21:19.570 --> 00:21:23.800
we were trying to solve these
day-to-day tough problems.

00:21:23.800 --> 00:21:27.200
So migration planning--
we already covered this,

00:21:27.200 --> 00:21:29.630
but plan twice, execute once.

00:21:29.630 --> 00:21:31.120
I cannot stress this enough.

00:21:31.120 --> 00:21:34.990
Now, what did it actually
mean when we did planning?

00:21:34.990 --> 00:21:37.240
We started with the
very, very basics.

00:21:37.240 --> 00:21:39.850
We put the people into
Qwiklabs and Coursera.

00:21:39.850 --> 00:21:43.060
We gave them access
to online training

00:21:43.060 --> 00:21:45.813
that they used to pick
up at least the basics

00:21:45.813 --> 00:21:47.230
of a new system
because most of us

00:21:47.230 --> 00:21:50.030
were actually unfamiliar
with GCP to begin with.

00:21:50.030 --> 00:21:52.030
Then, we went into the
next stage, which is, now

00:21:52.030 --> 00:21:53.680
that we understand
the basics, we

00:21:53.680 --> 00:21:56.320
started to work closely
with the Google TAMs

00:21:56.320 --> 00:22:00.250
to actually understand certain
technologies very, very deeply.

00:22:00.250 --> 00:22:02.350
We'd already identified
things like GKE.

00:22:02.350 --> 00:22:05.600
But it wasn't just enough that
we looked at the documentation.

00:22:05.600 --> 00:22:07.360
So we would sit
down with the TAMs

00:22:07.360 --> 00:22:10.150
and walk them through our
use cases across the board

00:22:10.150 --> 00:22:14.050
and explain how we were planning
to use it and what made sense.

00:22:14.050 --> 00:22:16.300
Then, we went even
one more level deeper.

00:22:16.300 --> 00:22:18.400
At this point, we had
each of the teams that

00:22:18.400 --> 00:22:21.700
owned pieces of the architecture
sit down for days on end

00:22:21.700 --> 00:22:22.990
with the Google TAMs.

00:22:22.990 --> 00:22:25.420
And the TAMs would bring
in their own experts

00:22:25.420 --> 00:22:28.060
based on specific technologies
that we wanted to use.

00:22:28.060 --> 00:22:33.120
If we needed a Postgres
equivalent, so Cloud SQL,

00:22:33.120 --> 00:22:34.620
they would bring
those experts over.

00:22:34.620 --> 00:22:36.870
If we were evaluating Pub/Sub,
then we would make sure

00:22:36.870 --> 00:22:38.140
we had a Pub/Sub expert there.

00:22:38.140 --> 00:22:40.250
And we would spend
all day in a room,

00:22:40.250 --> 00:22:43.500
walking through the stack,
breaking it down over and over,

00:22:43.500 --> 00:22:45.100
digging into how
they're supposed

00:22:45.100 --> 00:22:47.710
to talk to each other, how
the firewall rules work,

00:22:47.710 --> 00:22:49.720
how does access flow--

00:22:49.720 --> 00:22:51.580
everything from
where do we set up

00:22:51.580 --> 00:22:54.610
the projects to which
services run on which cluster.

00:22:54.610 --> 00:22:57.370
We tried to lay as
much out as possible.

00:22:57.370 --> 00:23:01.177
The advantage of doing this in
a very collaborative fashion as

00:23:01.177 --> 00:23:03.760
opposed to having people go out
and write documents and review

00:23:03.760 --> 00:23:07.150
it over and over is that
it really sped up the time

00:23:07.150 --> 00:23:08.960
to get to the ideal result.

00:23:08.960 --> 00:23:11.980
So we actually focused a lot on
just bringing people together.

00:23:11.980 --> 00:23:13.980
We actually had people
fly to different offices.

00:23:13.980 --> 00:23:16.510
We had local TAMs, and this
was another big advantage

00:23:16.510 --> 00:23:18.230
of local TAMs.

00:23:18.230 --> 00:23:21.760
Then, this actually gave us time
for learning and exploration.

00:23:21.760 --> 00:23:26.320
So as I mentioned, we covered
a lot of detailed content

00:23:26.320 --> 00:23:27.280
with the TAMs.

00:23:27.280 --> 00:23:29.920
Once we had gotten to the
point where we understood what

00:23:29.920 --> 00:23:32.500
each team had to do, we
went to the next level,

00:23:32.500 --> 00:23:35.000
which was we actually started
doing cross team dependencies.

00:23:35.000 --> 00:23:36.830
So we would have teams
sit down together

00:23:36.830 --> 00:23:39.250
where known dependencies
existed, once again,

00:23:39.250 --> 00:23:42.070
in collaboration with Google,
to talk through the details,

00:23:42.070 --> 00:23:44.020
like, OK, how do we
manage latency here?

00:23:44.020 --> 00:23:46.510
How do we manage
the data workloads

00:23:46.510 --> 00:23:49.060
that are going to flow
through at peak periods?

00:23:49.060 --> 00:23:51.430
This forces you to
do a lot of research.

00:23:51.430 --> 00:23:54.460
This forces you to gather the
right metrics, to understand

00:23:54.460 --> 00:23:55.820
your system better.

00:23:55.820 --> 00:23:58.780
It's not just enough for
you to have the basics.

00:23:58.780 --> 00:24:02.440
Everybody knows, well, how many
requesters their service deals

00:24:02.440 --> 00:24:06.490
with, but you don't necessarily
know what your error rate is,

00:24:06.490 --> 00:24:09.310
what your peak loads look like,
what patterns your traffic

00:24:09.310 --> 00:24:12.160
flows through, how does it
deal with specific types

00:24:12.160 --> 00:24:13.120
of exceptions.

00:24:13.120 --> 00:24:15.460
Because when you move from
one stack to the other,

00:24:15.460 --> 00:24:18.190
you're actually going to see a
change in where the errors are

00:24:18.190 --> 00:24:22.300
generated because different
clouds handle resiliency

00:24:22.300 --> 00:24:23.230
differently.

00:24:23.230 --> 00:24:25.880
So we really focused
on those aspects.

00:24:25.880 --> 00:24:27.890
Now, while the teams
are off doing that,

00:24:27.890 --> 00:24:29.630
we were focusing on phase 0.

00:24:29.630 --> 00:24:31.130
What is phase 0?

00:24:31.130 --> 00:24:34.970
Compliance, security, SRE,
network, and developer tools.

00:24:34.970 --> 00:24:37.160
So while the engineering
teams were off figuring out

00:24:37.160 --> 00:24:40.310
how they could migrate, we had
actually set off the phase 0

00:24:40.310 --> 00:24:42.330
teams, which, really,
if you think about it,

00:24:42.330 --> 00:24:43.460
this is your foundation.

00:24:43.460 --> 00:24:46.710
This is what creates the right
GCP foundation for you to use.

00:24:46.710 --> 00:24:49.610
We had these teams off
and running in parallel,

00:24:49.610 --> 00:24:53.210
setting up the actual stage
and production infrastructure.

00:24:53.210 --> 00:24:55.580
So the engineering teams
were off in sandboxes

00:24:55.580 --> 00:24:57.050
having these detailed
conversations

00:24:57.050 --> 00:24:58.520
and learning more
about the tech.

00:24:58.520 --> 00:25:00.727
And in the meantime,
security was coming up

00:25:00.727 --> 00:25:02.810
with how do you set up the
Google Groups to set up

00:25:02.810 --> 00:25:03.990
the right access.

00:25:03.990 --> 00:25:06.470
How do you do editor
versus read only?

00:25:06.470 --> 00:25:08.030
How do you do billing access?

00:25:08.030 --> 00:25:09.830
Where do you give super admin?

00:25:09.830 --> 00:25:11.870
How does SRE get
access to systems

00:25:11.870 --> 00:25:14.510
across different
organizational units

00:25:14.510 --> 00:25:17.210
at your company versus what
you do for your own teams

00:25:17.210 --> 00:25:19.940
within your own
organizational unit?

00:25:19.940 --> 00:25:21.770
We also covered compliance.

00:25:21.770 --> 00:25:24.670
GDPR was a big one that
we were dealing with.

00:25:24.670 --> 00:25:26.210
There's a lot of
little nitty gritty

00:25:26.210 --> 00:25:28.127
that you figure out as
you're digging into it.

00:25:28.127 --> 00:25:30.830
For example, GCP,
by default, does

00:25:30.830 --> 00:25:33.428
geoblocking of certain
countries that are embargo.

00:25:33.428 --> 00:25:34.970
And these are pieces
that you're only

00:25:34.970 --> 00:25:37.428
going to figure out when your
compliance team gets in there

00:25:37.428 --> 00:25:40.130
and asks the right questions
because that's what they do.

00:25:40.130 --> 00:25:43.490
At the same time, network
was heavy, heavy focus.

00:25:43.490 --> 00:25:45.980
We pushed bandwidth like crazy.

00:25:45.980 --> 00:25:48.832
So we definitely had the
network team in there,

00:25:48.832 --> 00:25:51.290
not just looking at how they
were going to use the existing

00:25:51.290 --> 00:25:54.110
setup, but how we can
improve it and actually build

00:25:54.110 --> 00:25:56.130
a much stronger
foundation for what

00:25:56.130 --> 00:25:58.130
we knew we were going to
scale in the next three

00:25:58.130 --> 00:26:00.200
to four years.

00:26:00.200 --> 00:26:03.620
Now, workstreams and
dependencies-- so this

00:26:03.620 --> 00:26:05.880
is a tricky one to
talk through, but I'm

00:26:05.880 --> 00:26:07.380
going to hand-wave
my way through it

00:26:07.380 --> 00:26:09.047
and hopefully you
guys are going to see.

00:26:09.047 --> 00:26:11.930
Because this was actually a
critical part of our success.

00:26:11.930 --> 00:26:14.870
What we focused on in this
whole pre-migration area

00:26:14.870 --> 00:26:16.820
was building out workstreams.

00:26:16.820 --> 00:26:20.330
Think of your workstreams
as layers within your stack.

00:26:20.330 --> 00:26:22.850
At the very basic, you're
probably going to have network.

00:26:22.850 --> 00:26:24.350
Then on top of that,
you're probably

00:26:24.350 --> 00:26:26.840
going to have your
foundational pieces of how

00:26:26.840 --> 00:26:28.147
do you start up the clusters.

00:26:28.147 --> 00:26:30.230
Then you're going to have
the actual services that

00:26:30.230 --> 00:26:31.200
run between them.

00:26:31.200 --> 00:26:33.260
But the services are
also layered, right?

00:26:33.260 --> 00:26:35.000
You will have your
backend services

00:26:35.000 --> 00:26:37.640
that are talking to the data
pipelines, that are actually

00:26:37.640 --> 00:26:40.820
talking to a middleware, that
are talking to a pure frontend

00:26:40.820 --> 00:26:41.550
service.

00:26:41.550 --> 00:26:43.920
So there's all these
layers that you have.

00:26:43.920 --> 00:26:46.130
Now, if you're able
to neatly draw them

00:26:46.130 --> 00:26:50.450
out so that they create
these parallel paths, what

00:26:50.450 --> 00:26:54.200
you've effectively done is, one,
you've identified workstreams.

00:26:54.200 --> 00:26:56.510
Each of these workstreams
is a cohesive unit

00:26:56.510 --> 00:26:57.980
that can move independently.

00:26:57.980 --> 00:27:00.470
Your data pipeline is
different from your services,

00:27:00.470 --> 00:27:02.210
is different from your frontend.

00:27:02.210 --> 00:27:03.585
The other thing
that you're going

00:27:03.585 --> 00:27:05.990
to end up doing when
you draw this out,

00:27:05.990 --> 00:27:08.930
you're going to have lines that
crossover between workstreams.

00:27:08.930 --> 00:27:10.550
Those are your dependencies.

00:27:10.550 --> 00:27:14.060
So now you know exactly who
talks to whom because you've

00:27:14.060 --> 00:27:16.220
leaned this out in charts.

00:27:16.220 --> 00:27:19.040
Towards the end,
we had giant charts

00:27:19.040 --> 00:27:23.190
that had a ton of different
workstreams in them.

00:27:23.190 --> 00:27:26.750
And so we actually ended
up creating workstreams

00:27:26.750 --> 00:27:28.550
within workstreams
in some cases.

00:27:28.550 --> 00:27:31.732
But when you're doing a
migration as a company,

00:27:31.732 --> 00:27:32.690
you have the bandwidth.

00:27:32.690 --> 00:27:35.460
You have the manpower to be
able to go and break it down.

00:27:35.460 --> 00:27:37.160
So what we did was
each workstream

00:27:37.160 --> 00:27:39.745
that we started with
was a business unit.

00:27:39.745 --> 00:27:41.120
And then that
business unit would

00:27:41.120 --> 00:27:44.120
go in and break down their
workstream into further layers.

00:27:44.120 --> 00:27:45.950
But these layers
were very important.

00:27:45.950 --> 00:27:50.090
One, we always had visibility
into what the layers were

00:27:50.090 --> 00:27:51.860
and where the dependencies lay.

00:27:51.860 --> 00:27:56.210
Two, we never ran into
the issue of people

00:27:56.210 --> 00:27:58.760
not understanding how the
architecture was flowing

00:27:58.760 --> 00:28:01.670
because this was always a great
reference point for us to be

00:28:01.670 --> 00:28:04.200
able to lay back to.

00:28:04.200 --> 00:28:06.180
Finally, tracking
the milestones--

00:28:06.180 --> 00:28:09.330
so after we have these
workstreams and dependencies

00:28:09.330 --> 00:28:12.610
laid out, it actually let
us track two milestones.

00:28:12.610 --> 00:28:15.000
Now, I'm going to say this
to you a little differently

00:28:15.000 --> 00:28:17.730
because a lot of us, when
we're doing this migration,

00:28:17.730 --> 00:28:19.950
we're going to focus
on the process,

00:28:19.950 --> 00:28:21.940
and we're going to
focus on the deadlines.

00:28:21.940 --> 00:28:22.980
That's natural.

00:28:22.980 --> 00:28:24.190
That's what we do.

00:28:24.190 --> 00:28:25.920
But when we have a
global set of teams

00:28:25.920 --> 00:28:29.048
and our goal is to
empower them, there

00:28:29.048 --> 00:28:30.840
was no way for us to
be able to pull it off

00:28:30.840 --> 00:28:33.330
by saying, hey, this is the
date you got to hit it by,

00:28:33.330 --> 00:28:35.650
these are all the processes
that you have to follow.

00:28:35.650 --> 00:28:37.470
So we kind of flipped
it on its head.

00:28:37.470 --> 00:28:40.140
We focused on
milestones, not status.

00:28:40.140 --> 00:28:42.690
And we focused on
results, not process.

00:28:42.690 --> 00:28:45.030
So what that means
is every team was

00:28:45.030 --> 00:28:48.180
able to own a piece of the
workstream independently.

00:28:48.180 --> 00:28:50.460
All we did was create
standard milestones

00:28:50.460 --> 00:28:52.560
that we could track
across these workstreams.

00:28:52.560 --> 00:28:54.727
This is something as
simple as your stage prod

00:28:54.727 --> 00:28:57.060
being milestones or a security
review being a milestone.

00:28:57.060 --> 00:28:58.620
Like, pick your
milestones, right?

00:28:58.620 --> 00:29:00.900
But they become standard
across the teams.

00:29:00.900 --> 00:29:03.210
The second thing
we did was, when

00:29:03.210 --> 00:29:06.870
we would collaborate, when
we would review updates,

00:29:06.870 --> 00:29:08.310
we never focused on the status.

00:29:08.310 --> 00:29:09.570
We focused on the results.

00:29:09.570 --> 00:29:11.130
We focused on the milestones.

00:29:11.130 --> 00:29:12.930
So what have you
achieved so far and what

00:29:12.930 --> 00:29:13.972
are you going after next?

00:29:13.972 --> 00:29:15.490
How can we help you get there?

00:29:15.490 --> 00:29:18.270
Where do the dependencies
exist, and how can we

00:29:18.270 --> 00:29:20.283
help bring the
collaboration together?

00:29:20.283 --> 00:29:22.200
What it did was it kind
of changed the framing

00:29:22.200 --> 00:29:23.430
of this whole migration.

00:29:23.430 --> 00:29:28.920
It became less of a process and
it became more of a shared goal

00:29:28.920 --> 00:29:30.630
that we were all going after.

00:29:30.630 --> 00:29:33.660
The other thing that was unique,
at least in our situation,

00:29:33.660 --> 00:29:37.090
was our teams used
different supporting stacks.

00:29:37.090 --> 00:29:40.860
So not everybody is on
the same ticketing system,

00:29:40.860 --> 00:29:44.070
not everybody is using
the same developer tools.

00:29:44.070 --> 00:29:45.990
So once again, in
that case, how are we

00:29:45.990 --> 00:29:47.400
going to actually track to this?

00:29:47.400 --> 00:29:52.110
How are we going to know when
an engineering team has finished

00:29:52.110 --> 00:29:54.880
x milestone because how the
traditional process grows,

00:29:54.880 --> 00:29:55.380
right?

00:29:55.380 --> 00:29:57.000
You put everything into Jira.

00:29:57.000 --> 00:29:58.243
You create this giant chart.

00:29:58.243 --> 00:30:00.410
And then everybody's looking
at it every week going,

00:30:00.410 --> 00:30:02.540
oh, we slipped by two days here.

00:30:02.540 --> 00:30:04.123
But that was not
what we wanted to do.

00:30:04.123 --> 00:30:06.082
Because, again, the focus
was not the deadline.

00:30:06.082 --> 00:30:07.960
The focus was the shared
vision, this goal.

00:30:07.960 --> 00:30:11.160
So we actually took that
work into, believe it or not,

00:30:11.160 --> 00:30:14.520
we spent a week creating
this tracker that essentially

00:30:14.520 --> 00:30:16.920
took all these
disparate pieces of data

00:30:16.920 --> 00:30:20.700
and just kind of rolled it up
into a simple workstream-based

00:30:20.700 --> 00:30:21.660
progress.

00:30:21.660 --> 00:30:23.903
And we shared that every
week across the board,

00:30:23.903 --> 00:30:25.320
the executives,
they all knew what

00:30:25.320 --> 00:30:29.470
was going on because it was
a very simple way to view it.

00:30:29.470 --> 00:30:31.650
In fact, I think-- and
I'll say that out loud--

00:30:31.650 --> 00:30:34.050
I believe Google
liked our tracker

00:30:34.050 --> 00:30:35.790
so much that it may
be showing up now

00:30:35.790 --> 00:30:37.780
as a template for
other migrations.

00:30:37.780 --> 00:30:40.110
So if you see one
that looks kind

00:30:40.110 --> 00:30:43.130
of like a set of
workstreams, that was us.

00:30:43.130 --> 00:30:45.810
And then, finally, the
cadence for the global teams--

00:30:45.810 --> 00:30:48.840
now, as I said, it's very hard
to track these global teams,

00:30:48.840 --> 00:30:51.830
so we focused on the
goals and the results.

00:30:51.830 --> 00:30:55.930
We tried really hard to
stay away from the process.

00:30:55.930 --> 00:30:57.540
So at the end of
the day, this is

00:30:57.540 --> 00:30:59.310
kind of a very, very
high level of what

00:30:59.310 --> 00:31:00.870
the workstreams look like.

00:31:00.870 --> 00:31:02.970
These cohorts are
essentially big milestones

00:31:02.970 --> 00:31:05.138
that each one of these
people owned delivering.

00:31:05.138 --> 00:31:06.930
And if you notice, it
was possible for them

00:31:06.930 --> 00:31:09.510
to be extremely parallel.

00:31:09.510 --> 00:31:12.330
Big ones we even broke down
into further workstreams.

00:31:12.330 --> 00:31:14.500
And we had dependencies
tracked between them.

00:31:14.500 --> 00:31:18.390
But the reason why we could
move on all of this in parallel

00:31:18.390 --> 00:31:21.150
was because we focused on
making these workstreams as

00:31:21.150 --> 00:31:22.490
independent as possible.

00:31:22.490 --> 00:31:24.870
SOM ROY: And I think
the cohort was also

00:31:24.870 --> 00:31:29.310
really defining what goes in
cohort one and two and three.

00:31:29.310 --> 00:31:30.810
That was really
also very important

00:31:30.810 --> 00:31:33.120
because it kind of gave
us a staggered approach.

00:31:33.120 --> 00:31:36.280
And when we went live,
it wasn't all or nothing.

00:31:36.280 --> 00:31:38.680
We went live at the first
set, everything worked well,

00:31:38.680 --> 00:31:39.900
we went live with
the second one.

00:31:39.900 --> 00:31:41.370
So I think that was
really, really useful.

00:31:41.370 --> 00:31:41.850
VENK SUBRAMANIAN: Yeah.

00:31:41.850 --> 00:31:44.350
And we're actually going to
talk about some of our learnings

00:31:44.350 --> 00:31:46.523
now because we also
stumbled along the way.

00:31:46.523 --> 00:31:47.940
There were things
that we learned.

00:31:47.940 --> 00:31:49.700
And they're kind of
the nitty gritty.

00:31:49.700 --> 00:31:52.300
They're the practicalities of
when you try to do a migration.

00:31:52.300 --> 00:31:55.110
So we'll start with the don'ts
because we always want to know

00:31:55.110 --> 00:31:56.790
the don'ts first.

00:31:56.790 --> 00:31:57.690
OK.

00:31:57.690 --> 00:32:00.880
First of all, don't migrate
all of your baggage.

00:32:00.880 --> 00:32:02.880
But also, don't migrate
none of your baggage.

00:32:02.880 --> 00:32:04.200
You've got to pick.

00:32:04.200 --> 00:32:06.600
Think about it like moving
into a new apartment.

00:32:06.600 --> 00:32:08.130
When you move into
a new apartment,

00:32:08.130 --> 00:32:10.110
you don't just take
all those boxes

00:32:10.110 --> 00:32:12.610
that have been sitting in your
garage for months or years.

00:32:12.610 --> 00:32:14.670
But you also don't just
leave them in the house.

00:32:14.670 --> 00:32:17.010
You want to go through
and clean up as you go.

00:32:17.010 --> 00:32:17.790
So do that.

00:32:17.790 --> 00:32:19.410
Be practical about it.

00:32:19.410 --> 00:32:21.790
And more importantly, don't
try to put a ton of process

00:32:21.790 --> 00:32:22.290
around it.

00:32:22.290 --> 00:32:24.090
Your engineering teams
know these things.

00:32:24.090 --> 00:32:25.770
They know where the
baggage practically

00:32:25.770 --> 00:32:27.600
can be moved versus not.

00:32:27.600 --> 00:32:29.910
Just let the engineering
teams be empowered

00:32:29.910 --> 00:32:31.320
to do something like this.

00:32:31.320 --> 00:32:33.750
Two, don't build a snowflake.

00:32:33.750 --> 00:32:36.690
If you have a snowflake,
don't migrate the snowflake.

00:32:36.690 --> 00:32:40.080
We all have stacks, especially
if the technology we're

00:32:40.080 --> 00:32:42.690
working on is a few
years old, we all

00:32:42.690 --> 00:32:44.490
have these snowflakes
sitting around.

00:32:44.490 --> 00:32:46.170
But the world has changed.

00:32:46.170 --> 00:32:48.300
So much is out there now.

00:32:48.300 --> 00:32:51.840
Google aggressively
looks across and tries

00:32:51.840 --> 00:32:53.890
to find these common patterns.

00:32:53.890 --> 00:32:56.670
So there are known
best cloud practices.

00:32:56.670 --> 00:32:59.250
There are known application
patterns to follow.

00:32:59.250 --> 00:33:00.220
Use them.

00:33:00.220 --> 00:33:02.100
This is actually a
great time for you

00:33:02.100 --> 00:33:04.830
to be able to reinforce them.

00:33:04.830 --> 00:33:08.220
You know that there is
a VM out there somewhere

00:33:08.220 --> 00:33:10.470
that is open to the internet
and nobody knows about.

00:33:10.470 --> 00:33:12.540
You know there is
that one guy whose

00:33:12.540 --> 00:33:15.180
laptop has access to the
production infrastructure.

00:33:15.180 --> 00:33:16.710
Take the time to clean it up.

00:33:16.710 --> 00:33:19.980
Take the time to put
better practices in place.

00:33:19.980 --> 00:33:21.990
Don't migrate the monolith.

00:33:21.990 --> 00:33:25.530
Now, this is a harder problem
than just putting it up

00:33:25.530 --> 00:33:27.280
as a point on a slide.

00:33:27.280 --> 00:33:30.840
So when you're
looking at a monolith,

00:33:30.840 --> 00:33:33.540
do your best to apply
the strangler pattern.

00:33:33.540 --> 00:33:36.690
Pull out what you can that's
safe, what's feasible,

00:33:36.690 --> 00:33:37.875
and try to move that over.

00:33:37.875 --> 00:33:39.750
It's actually going to
have a twofold result.

00:33:39.750 --> 00:33:42.240
One, it's going to make your
monolith smaller and easier

00:33:42.240 --> 00:33:43.080
to migrate.

00:33:43.080 --> 00:33:46.410
But two, it's also going to
provide a proof of concept

00:33:46.410 --> 00:33:49.950
for how to be able to
scale to bigger migrations

00:33:49.950 --> 00:33:52.620
because you're going to pull
out really, really small pieces.

00:33:52.620 --> 00:33:54.000
You can start with
simple things,

00:33:54.000 --> 00:33:57.060
like your config service,
or your identity, or just

00:33:57.060 --> 00:33:58.740
a connector to the database.

00:33:58.740 --> 00:34:00.510
Separate out your
database through an API

00:34:00.510 --> 00:34:03.400
and just move that over.

00:34:03.400 --> 00:34:05.760
Now, don't retain single
points of failure.

00:34:05.760 --> 00:34:08.909
This one was especially
important for us.

00:34:08.909 --> 00:34:11.940
For example, I told you we used
network bandwidth like crazy.

00:34:11.940 --> 00:34:13.844
So when we were
moving over to Google,

00:34:13.844 --> 00:34:14.969
we really buttoned that up.

00:34:14.969 --> 00:34:18.239
We created multiple points
of failures and redundancies

00:34:18.239 --> 00:34:21.750
in there that has actually
allowed us, in recent times,

00:34:21.750 --> 00:34:26.010
to deal with network outages
that we've seen that were

00:34:26.010 --> 00:34:27.510
on the third party side.

00:34:27.510 --> 00:34:29.052
But we've been able
to deal with that

00:34:29.052 --> 00:34:31.560
because we actually took the
time to evaluate our bandwidth,

00:34:31.560 --> 00:34:35.370
understand it, and then put
the right redundancy in place.

00:34:35.370 --> 00:34:37.260
We did the same
thing for Kubernetes.

00:34:37.260 --> 00:34:39.070
Multi-zone's always
a great idea.

00:34:39.070 --> 00:34:40.590
Multi-region is even better.

00:34:40.590 --> 00:34:42.460
But multi-zones are
always a great idea,

00:34:42.460 --> 00:34:44.770
so we took the time
to do that too.

00:34:44.770 --> 00:34:47.639
And finally, don't
re-architect everything.

00:34:47.639 --> 00:34:49.560
Especially not your plumbing.

00:34:49.560 --> 00:34:52.139
Don't try to move over to that
brand new monitoring system

00:34:52.139 --> 00:34:54.120
while you're trying
to do a migration.

00:34:54.120 --> 00:34:57.630
Move parts over that need
to be migrated and deal

00:34:57.630 --> 00:34:59.760
with everything as a
separate initiative.

00:34:59.760 --> 00:35:03.300
Now in some cases, they may be
tied together, in which case,

00:35:03.300 --> 00:35:05.340
use your workstreams
and dependencies to be

00:35:05.340 --> 00:35:06.660
able to separate them out.

00:35:06.660 --> 00:35:08.460
If you treat your
developer tools

00:35:08.460 --> 00:35:13.417
as a later workstreams
have a dependency on,

00:35:13.417 --> 00:35:15.750
you're actually going to
create a phased approach of how

00:35:15.750 --> 00:35:18.530
we're doing things.

00:35:18.530 --> 00:35:20.630
SOM ROY: So, like
Venk talked about,

00:35:20.630 --> 00:35:24.145
the very specific on
the technology side,

00:35:24.145 --> 00:35:26.270
I'm going to talk about
the people and process side

00:35:26.270 --> 00:35:29.720
as well because, until all these
three together come together,

00:35:29.720 --> 00:35:31.800
you won't have a
successful migration.

00:35:31.800 --> 00:35:35.090
So the first key don't on
the people process side

00:35:35.090 --> 00:35:37.130
is please don't
migrate in a vacuum.

00:35:37.130 --> 00:35:39.170
Think about the
downstream dependencies.

00:35:39.170 --> 00:35:42.830
Think about what and
other teams downstream

00:35:42.830 --> 00:35:45.500
are going to be impacted
by this migration.

00:35:45.500 --> 00:35:49.040
And please involve all these
cross-functional stakeholders

00:35:49.040 --> 00:35:49.670
when you do so.

00:35:49.670 --> 00:35:53.780
If you don't do that, it's like,
even though your component is

00:35:53.780 --> 00:35:56.660
successfully migrated, the
shared vision will not be met.

00:35:56.660 --> 00:36:00.450
So no migration in a vacuum.

00:36:00.450 --> 00:36:02.660
I think, don't
focus on deadlines.

00:36:02.660 --> 00:36:03.890
I think Venk talked about it.

00:36:03.890 --> 00:36:05.432
I'm not going to go
into the details.

00:36:05.432 --> 00:36:07.880
I think it should
be shared goals.

00:36:07.880 --> 00:36:10.760
It should be talking
about shared focus areas.

00:36:10.760 --> 00:36:14.870
And everybody should feel
as part of the migration

00:36:14.870 --> 00:36:19.287
and just saying, I have
to do it by October 30,

00:36:19.287 --> 00:36:20.870
that's not going to
cut it because you

00:36:20.870 --> 00:36:24.350
have to take everybody along.

00:36:24.350 --> 00:36:27.500
Another thing which is
important is the lift and shift

00:36:27.500 --> 00:36:28.790
versus transformation.

00:36:28.790 --> 00:36:32.180
I think what the point
we are trying to make

00:36:32.180 --> 00:36:34.340
is there is no one
correct answer.

00:36:34.340 --> 00:36:36.560
Some of your components
will be lift and shift.

00:36:36.560 --> 00:36:39.200
It makes sense to just
take what you're running

00:36:39.200 --> 00:36:40.850
and just move it to GCP.

00:36:40.850 --> 00:36:44.510
Versus some, you
should take the option

00:36:44.510 --> 00:36:47.910
that you are doing a migration,
why don't you transform?

00:36:47.910 --> 00:36:50.510
So each workstream should
be treated very differently.

00:36:50.510 --> 00:36:53.420
Each component should be
different treated and evaluated

00:36:53.420 --> 00:36:54.500
differently.

00:36:54.500 --> 00:36:56.930
Across all the
workstreams, I think,

00:36:56.930 --> 00:36:58.580
there is no mandate
that everything

00:36:58.580 --> 00:37:00.890
needs to be either lift
and shift or transform.

00:37:00.890 --> 00:37:02.930
I think the Unity's
migration was

00:37:02.930 --> 00:37:05.990
a very good example of a mix
of both these approaches, where

00:37:05.990 --> 00:37:07.470
it made sense.

00:37:07.470 --> 00:37:10.350
We had a lift and shift
where it made sense.

00:37:10.350 --> 00:37:13.340
We actually looked at a
transformation approach.

00:37:13.340 --> 00:37:17.990
And then, just don't assume
that planning leads to success.

00:37:17.990 --> 00:37:19.730
While the planning
is really important,

00:37:19.730 --> 00:37:22.100
but following up
and executing on it

00:37:22.100 --> 00:37:23.870
is really, really important.

00:37:23.870 --> 00:37:26.120
And iterate on the plan.

00:37:26.120 --> 00:37:28.850
In every migration
that we are seeing,

00:37:28.850 --> 00:37:31.100
there are unknown blockers
that will crop up.

00:37:31.100 --> 00:37:35.690
There will be churn in terms
of folks joining the team.

00:37:35.690 --> 00:37:38.810
So you have to keep
iterating on the plan

00:37:38.810 --> 00:37:40.580
because if you don't
iterate on the plan

00:37:40.580 --> 00:37:42.247
and you just stick
to something that you

00:37:42.247 --> 00:37:46.080
planned six months back, that's
not setting up for success.

00:37:46.080 --> 00:37:48.560
So that is a really,
really key don't in terms

00:37:48.560 --> 00:37:51.401
of from a process perspective.

00:37:51.401 --> 00:37:54.480
VENK SUBRAMANIAN: So now, what
to do from the technology side.

00:37:54.480 --> 00:37:56.190
We've covered phase 0 in detail.

00:37:56.190 --> 00:37:58.770
It is very important to
establish a solid GCP

00:37:58.770 --> 00:38:00.270
foundation.

00:38:00.270 --> 00:38:02.490
Because here's the thing
to remember-- the migration

00:38:02.490 --> 00:38:03.740
is not the end of the journey.

00:38:03.740 --> 00:38:04.950
It's the beginning of it.

00:38:04.950 --> 00:38:08.190
You are tying yourself to a
technology for a long period

00:38:08.190 --> 00:38:09.795
of time, hopefully.

00:38:09.795 --> 00:38:13.520
Hopefully migration is not
what you do as a company.

00:38:13.520 --> 00:38:16.020
And when you're doing that,
you want the foundation

00:38:16.020 --> 00:38:17.880
to be extremely solid.

00:38:17.880 --> 00:38:20.340
The more debt you
accumulate, the harder

00:38:20.340 --> 00:38:21.660
it will be going forward.

00:38:21.660 --> 00:38:23.430
Now, some kinds of
debt are isolated,

00:38:23.430 --> 00:38:25.710
and you're going to
actually make calls on them.

00:38:25.710 --> 00:38:27.930
But foundational
debt is different.

00:38:27.930 --> 00:38:31.320
If your access controls are not
in place when five people are

00:38:31.320 --> 00:38:34.200
in the system, it is going
to be a lot harder to do it

00:38:34.200 --> 00:38:37.410
when 300 or 3,000 people are
going to be in the system.

00:38:37.410 --> 00:38:39.630
If your network
firewalls aren't in place

00:38:39.630 --> 00:38:42.400
to segregate the
environments when there

00:38:42.400 --> 00:38:44.670
is no services in
there, it's not

00:38:44.670 --> 00:38:47.220
going to happen when there's
thousands of services in there.

00:38:47.220 --> 00:38:50.250
And what also changes
is the manpower

00:38:50.250 --> 00:38:53.280
needed to do it at a later
time exponentially goes up too.

00:38:53.280 --> 00:38:54.900
So it is very, very
important that you

00:38:54.900 --> 00:38:57.270
establish this foundation.

00:38:57.270 --> 00:39:01.780
Automate everything--
this is the key part.

00:39:01.780 --> 00:39:04.890
So we actually took this
time to automate everything

00:39:04.890 --> 00:39:06.570
that we found that was manual.

00:39:06.570 --> 00:39:09.250
And we actually had it in
three different styles.

00:39:09.250 --> 00:39:11.790
So everything was
infrastructure as code.

00:39:11.790 --> 00:39:15.510
That was kind of the
principle that we aligned on

00:39:15.510 --> 00:39:16.840
that we were going to do.

00:39:16.840 --> 00:39:18.840
But also, when we were
looking at infrastructure

00:39:18.840 --> 00:39:20.730
and we realized all
infrastructure was being

00:39:20.730 --> 00:39:24.600
written as code, we
centralized a lot of the pieces

00:39:24.600 --> 00:39:28.188
that we knew were in use across
multiple parts of the company

00:39:28.188 --> 00:39:29.730
and we offered them
as infrastructure

00:39:29.730 --> 00:39:33.013
as a service or
infrastructure as a framework.

00:39:33.013 --> 00:39:34.680
And what infrastructure
as a framework--

00:39:34.680 --> 00:39:36.750
I'm assuming all of
infrastructure as a service.

00:39:36.750 --> 00:39:38.960
But what infrastructure
as a framework does

00:39:38.960 --> 00:39:41.670
is it actually take some of
the burden of maintaining

00:39:41.670 --> 00:39:45.490
the service and passes that on
to the user of that service.

00:39:45.490 --> 00:39:47.670
And this works really
well for internal teams.

00:39:47.670 --> 00:39:52.230
So as a good example, we had
many, many uses of Mongo.

00:39:52.230 --> 00:39:54.990
And they were all isolated, some
of them were manually set up,

00:39:54.990 --> 00:39:57.800
some of them were set up in
different kinds of automation,

00:39:57.800 --> 00:40:00.480
and they all had different
configuration, different use

00:40:00.480 --> 00:40:01.127
cases.

00:40:01.127 --> 00:40:03.210
But we spent a time to
have a single team actually

00:40:03.210 --> 00:40:06.180
pick up all of that, understand
from each of the users

00:40:06.180 --> 00:40:08.220
what they were trying
to do, take it all back,

00:40:08.220 --> 00:40:10.960
and rewrite it as a
centralized module.

00:40:10.960 --> 00:40:13.950
Now, this module is a lot better
maintained because we actually

00:40:13.950 --> 00:40:18.010
use automation to not
just deploy and run it

00:40:18.010 --> 00:40:19.920
but we also use
automation to test it.

00:40:19.920 --> 00:40:22.650
We have a team that's dedicated
to continue to improve it,

00:40:22.650 --> 00:40:24.870
which means that you don't
fall behind on versions.

00:40:24.870 --> 00:40:26.700
You don't fall behind
on new functionality.

00:40:26.700 --> 00:40:30.460
You don't have bad bugs
sitting around in your system.

00:40:30.460 --> 00:40:32.910
But also, it didn't make
sense for a team like this

00:40:32.910 --> 00:40:35.250
to try to run every
database in the company.

00:40:35.250 --> 00:40:39.900
You're just creating just an
unhealthy central dependency.

00:40:39.900 --> 00:40:41.790
So we offered it
as a framework out.

00:40:41.790 --> 00:40:45.120
We maintain the module, we
improve it, you go use it.

00:40:45.120 --> 00:40:47.310
You own the cluster
that it runs on.

00:40:47.310 --> 00:40:50.400
It also passes the ball a little
bit of making sure everybody

00:40:50.400 --> 00:40:52.530
knows how to run infrastructure.

00:40:52.530 --> 00:40:55.420
Most of us today understand
the concept of, you build it,

00:40:55.420 --> 00:40:58.470
you run it, you own it.

00:40:58.470 --> 00:41:01.150
In fact, at Unity, we have this
joke that says, you build it,

00:41:01.150 --> 00:41:02.287
you run it, you pay for it.

00:41:02.287 --> 00:41:03.870
Because our engineering
teams actually

00:41:03.870 --> 00:41:07.470
know what it takes to
actually-- in terms of

00:41:07.470 --> 00:41:09.240
spend for their services to run.

00:41:09.240 --> 00:41:12.790
They use that to continuously
optimize their services.

00:41:12.790 --> 00:41:14.280
And this is the
kind of empowerment

00:41:14.280 --> 00:41:17.280
that you want to make sure
that you offer to your teams.

00:41:17.280 --> 00:41:19.320
Minimize technical
debt-- now, we've

00:41:19.320 --> 00:41:22.230
talked about technical
debt quite a bit.

00:41:22.230 --> 00:41:25.905
Specifically, what
you want to look at

00:41:25.905 --> 00:41:27.780
is just old debt that's
sitting around that's

00:41:27.780 --> 00:41:29.070
going to severely hamper you.

00:41:29.070 --> 00:41:32.220
In one of our cases, we had
a old, highly customized load

00:41:32.220 --> 00:41:34.440
balancer that had
been written in house

00:41:34.440 --> 00:41:35.813
that was sitting on a system.

00:41:35.813 --> 00:41:37.230
And when we looked
at GKE, we knew

00:41:37.230 --> 00:41:39.313
that we could re-architect
the system a little bit

00:41:39.313 --> 00:41:41.397
and actually get rid of
this custom load balancer.

00:41:41.397 --> 00:41:42.700
So we took the time to do it.

00:41:42.700 --> 00:41:45.450
So minimize technical
debt where feasible,

00:41:45.450 --> 00:41:47.160
especially fundamental
technical debt.

00:41:47.160 --> 00:41:48.810
Don't take it with you.

00:41:48.810 --> 00:41:51.960
Iterate and learn-- so the way
the workstreams were set up,

00:41:51.960 --> 00:41:54.480
they were actually set up in
increasing levels of complexity

00:41:54.480 --> 00:41:55.720
and collaboration.

00:41:55.720 --> 00:41:58.367
So the first systems to go
out were extremely simple.

00:41:58.367 --> 00:42:00.450
Most of them didn't even
have a back end database.

00:42:00.450 --> 00:42:02.190
It was like a GCS.

00:42:02.190 --> 00:42:05.580
But after a while, we started
to get into bigger services,

00:42:05.580 --> 00:42:07.680
but those that were
completely independent.

00:42:07.680 --> 00:42:09.670
Then you start introducing
a dependency layer.

00:42:09.670 --> 00:42:11.295
So now you have
multiple systems trying

00:42:11.295 --> 00:42:12.930
to go live at the same time.

00:42:12.930 --> 00:42:16.440
So we did it slowly with the
goal of accelerating closely

00:42:16.440 --> 00:42:18.570
towards the end
because you start

00:42:18.570 --> 00:42:22.000
to gain momentum once confidence
builds from the deployment.

00:42:22.000 --> 00:42:25.392
So our migration actually
looked like almost no work done

00:42:25.392 --> 00:42:27.600
within the first three months
because we were focused

00:42:27.600 --> 00:42:29.790
on the learnings
and the migration,

00:42:29.790 --> 00:42:32.085
very, very minimal services
migrated over the next two

00:42:32.085 --> 00:42:33.960
months because we were
getting our confidence

00:42:33.960 --> 00:42:36.180
and understanding how
everything worked in stage.

00:42:36.180 --> 00:42:38.460
And then, all of a sudden,
you start seeing you

00:42:38.460 --> 00:42:40.725
high-scale services just
ramping up really quickly,

00:42:40.725 --> 00:42:42.600
and all of that happened
within the last two,

00:42:42.600 --> 00:42:43.860
maybe three months.

00:42:43.860 --> 00:42:46.632
And finally, train,
plan, and prepare--

00:42:46.632 --> 00:42:48.090
the reason why I'm
calling this out

00:42:48.090 --> 00:42:50.660
is, separate the
artificial pressure

00:42:50.660 --> 00:42:53.360
of learning from execution.

00:42:53.360 --> 00:42:55.070
As an engineer, you know this.

00:42:55.070 --> 00:42:57.380
When you are told to
practically learn on the go

00:42:57.380 --> 00:43:00.560
or you're being asked to deliver
something that's brand new,

00:43:00.560 --> 00:43:03.050
it creates this
artificial pressure of,

00:43:03.050 --> 00:43:05.218
I need to do two things
at the same time.

00:43:05.218 --> 00:43:07.760
So we just set it up in a way
that it took that pressure off,

00:43:07.760 --> 00:43:10.260
the teams were able to focus
on learning and training first,

00:43:10.260 --> 00:43:13.056
and then when they were ready to
go, they were able to execute.

00:43:16.288 --> 00:43:18.580
SOM ROY: And finally, from
the people and process side,

00:43:18.580 --> 00:43:20.440
what are the things
you should do?

00:43:20.440 --> 00:43:23.650
I think this is very,
very like what we covered

00:43:23.650 --> 00:43:26.360
in the don'ts is, don't
start in a vacuum.

00:43:26.360 --> 00:43:28.870
So you should align on
overall vision and goals

00:43:28.870 --> 00:43:31.660
for GCP migration.

00:43:31.660 --> 00:43:34.630
Gaps in understanding,
it has to be

00:43:34.630 --> 00:43:36.520
top-down as well as bottom-up.

00:43:36.520 --> 00:43:38.710
Like, if the engineer
who is actually

00:43:38.710 --> 00:43:42.400
working on the
migration doesn't align

00:43:42.400 --> 00:43:45.790
to what the vision of the
company is to why big GCP

00:43:45.790 --> 00:43:47.410
and where the
migration is going,

00:43:47.410 --> 00:43:50.710
that leads to issues and
conflicts unnecessarily

00:43:50.710 --> 00:43:52.120
and it will delay
your migration.

00:43:52.120 --> 00:43:54.550
So then alignment
across top-down,

00:43:54.550 --> 00:43:57.360
bottoms-up is really,
really important.

00:43:57.360 --> 00:43:58.930
Establish a migration PMO.

00:43:58.930 --> 00:44:02.410
This may sound like a really,
really old school term, PMO.

00:44:02.410 --> 00:44:04.240
But project
management office does

00:44:04.240 --> 00:44:06.610
help when you are
doing a large migration

00:44:06.610 --> 00:44:10.240
with multiple workstreams with
so many different stakeholders

00:44:10.240 --> 00:44:10.750
involved.

00:44:10.750 --> 00:44:14.440
And absolutely identify
the right stakeholders,

00:44:14.440 --> 00:44:18.040
both from the Google side as
well as from the Unity side.

00:44:18.040 --> 00:44:20.020
That's really, really important.

00:44:20.020 --> 00:44:22.600
Identify clear owners
because responsibility

00:44:22.600 --> 00:44:25.330
and accountability, again,
a very old school term.

00:44:25.330 --> 00:44:26.270
Who's responsible?

00:44:26.270 --> 00:44:29.170
Who's accountable for the
success of that workstream?

00:44:29.170 --> 00:44:33.970
That is really
important to identify.

00:44:33.970 --> 00:44:36.280
The fourth one is, I
think, the one that is

00:44:36.280 --> 00:44:39.610
really close my heart, which is
set realistic migration goals.

00:44:39.610 --> 00:44:42.910
Aspiration versus reality,
like we keep talking about,

00:44:42.910 --> 00:44:46.360
yes, let's use this migration
to do something dramatic.

00:44:46.360 --> 00:44:49.180
Your entire stack
will change, something

00:44:49.180 --> 00:44:51.790
will change drastically
versus, let's

00:44:51.790 --> 00:44:54.257
be real in what can be achieved
in the six months, seven

00:44:54.257 --> 00:44:56.590
months or one year of migration
that you're going to do.

00:44:56.590 --> 00:44:59.110
So this alignment is
really, really important.

00:44:59.110 --> 00:45:01.670
And you as a company
need to take a call

00:45:01.670 --> 00:45:04.840
in what is the realistic goals.

00:45:04.840 --> 00:45:06.640
Establish and track milestones--

00:45:06.640 --> 00:45:10.170
I think Venk talked about-- and
I'm not going into the details

00:45:10.170 --> 00:45:11.920
again because you don't
have so much time.

00:45:11.920 --> 00:45:14.710
But have proper
tracking and make sure

00:45:14.710 --> 00:45:17.590
that you're looking at it on
a weekly, bi-weekly, monthly

00:45:17.590 --> 00:45:18.920
cadence.

00:45:18.920 --> 00:45:21.028
Prioritize for risk
issues and features,

00:45:21.028 --> 00:45:22.570
and this goes back
to the slide where

00:45:22.570 --> 00:45:25.510
we talked about all the feature
requests that Unity had.

00:45:25.510 --> 00:45:29.170
Like, please identify what
is P0 and P1s for you.

00:45:29.170 --> 00:45:32.960
What is P2 and P3, and can it
wait for six months, one year?

00:45:32.960 --> 00:45:35.740
Please do try to
actively identify.

00:45:35.740 --> 00:45:38.560
It always works better both
for Google and the customer

00:45:38.560 --> 00:45:40.630
if we have a prioritized list.

00:45:40.630 --> 00:45:42.610
And on the last point,
Venk, do you want to--

00:45:42.610 --> 00:45:43.420
I know this is--

00:45:43.420 --> 00:45:43.660
VENK SUBRAMANIAN: Yeah.

00:45:43.660 --> 00:45:45.493
This is the one I really
want to hammer home

00:45:45.493 --> 00:45:47.740
because a lot of you
are going to treat

00:45:47.740 --> 00:45:51.080
a migration as a project
that needs to be done.

00:45:51.080 --> 00:45:53.870
But it's a unique event,
and it's an important event.

00:45:53.870 --> 00:45:55.968
It actually sets the
stage for your company.

00:45:55.968 --> 00:45:58.010
And it's not going to
happen without your people.

00:45:58.010 --> 00:46:00.010
It's not going to happen
without your engineers.

00:46:00.010 --> 00:46:01.420
So celebrate success.

00:46:01.420 --> 00:46:04.540
We talk about people, process,
technology quite a bit.

00:46:04.540 --> 00:46:06.760
But if you think of it in
terms of people, planning,

00:46:06.760 --> 00:46:09.340
and empowerment, you're going
to get the right technology

00:46:09.340 --> 00:46:11.080
as a byproduct of that.

00:46:11.080 --> 00:46:14.470
If you focus on letting your
smart engineers be empowered

00:46:14.470 --> 00:46:16.690
and then get out of the
way and focus instead

00:46:16.690 --> 00:46:19.690
on how you can unblock them
and how you can help them get

00:46:19.690 --> 00:46:21.992
to the right kind of
tracking and milestones,

00:46:21.992 --> 00:46:23.950
they're going to get the
right results for you.

00:46:23.950 --> 00:46:25.540
Deadlines are never the focus.

00:46:25.540 --> 00:46:29.225
The goals and the vision are.

00:46:29.225 --> 00:46:30.100
And that's it for us.

00:46:30.100 --> 00:46:33.450
[MUSIC PLAYING]

